            r1_brief: `10 a.m. Casa Pardinilla serves some 30,000 covers a year: 40% from January to May, 36% from June to September and 24% from October to December. Each cover orders one main and spends €8.50 on starters and desserts. The 2026 carta (price, true raw-material cost, share of the mains): roast ternasco €21.00, €8.40, 18%; house hamburger €14.00, €4.83, 22%; huevos rotos with ham €12.50, €3.25, 16%; bacalao al ajoarriero €19.50, €6.45, 7%; beef entrecôte €26.00, €12.48, 9%; merluza a la romana €17.00, €7.65, 8%; vegetable lasagne €13.00, €3.90, 5%; creamy mushroom rice €16.50, €5.10, 15%. With the usual practice the year is projected at €75,322 of profit, a food cost of 34.3% and €10.75 of contribution per main. The costing: an escandallo for every dish with a standard recipe, the yield of each ingredient and current prices (€1,200 of your time and that of Benito Abadías, the chef); last year's cards, as now; the price per kilo on the supplier's invoice, without the yield; or Benito's estimate. And the reading of the carta, which also sets what the dining room recommends from tomorrow: the menu-engineering matrix, each dish's contribution against its share of sales (€600); the sales ranking, as now; each dish's food-cost percentage; or the prices of the restaurant across the street.`,
